# GMAT Focus Verbal Reasoning: reading comprehension (shared passages) and
# critical reasoning (argument in `passage`). V(domain, difficulty, skill,
# stem, options with the key first, explanation, passage= or stim=).
# Per test: 13 RC (passages of 4, 3, 3, 3) and 10 CR, in presentation order.
RC, CR = "gmatRC", "gmatCR"

STIMULI = [
    {"id": "GMAT-RC1", "title": "Corporate venture capital", "text":
     "Large companies have long invested in young firms, but the motives behind these investments differ from those of independent venture capitalists. An independent fund succeeds only if its investments return more cash than they cost, usually within about a decade. A corporate venture arm, by contrast, often accepts a lower financial return in exchange for a strategic benefit: an early look at a technology that could reshape the parent company's market.\n\n"
     "This strategic motive has a cost that researchers have only recently measured. Startups backed by corporate investors tend to be valued more highly at each funding round, which looks like good news for founders. Yet several studies find that these startups are somewhat less likely to be acquired by a company other than their corporate backer. Rival buyers, it seems, hesitate to bid for a firm whose investor may already know its secrets or may hold the right to match any offer.\n\n"
     "Some founders respond by limiting what a corporate investor may see, refusing it a board seat or keeping key technical data out of its reach. Such limits protect a startup's options, but they also weaken the very relationship that made the corporate investor attractive, since much of the value a large company offers, such as access to its distribution network or its engineers, depends on close cooperation. The choice of investor, in short, is less a question of who offers the most money than of which future the founders want to keep open."},
    {"id": "GMAT-RC2", "title": "The honeybee waggle dance", "text":
     "For decades, biologists treated the honeybee waggle dance as a precise map. A forager returning to the hive runs a straight line whose angle from vertical gives the direction of a food source relative to the sun, and whose duration signals its distance. Recruits that follow the dance then fly out and, in principle, find the food.\n\n"
     "Field experiments have complicated this picture. When researchers disrupted dances by turning hives on their sides, so that dancers could not orient their runs, colonies in some landscapes gathered almost as much food as normal colonies. In landscapes where food was patchy and scarce, however, the disrupted colonies did markedly worse. The dance, it appears, matters most when good food is hard to find; in rich landscapes, recruits can do nearly as well by relying on scent and on their own searching.\n\n"
     "These findings do not mean the dance is a poor signal. Measurements show that individual dances are noisy, with successive runs pointing in slightly different directions, and that recruits often need several trips to locate a source. A colony, though, averages over many dancers and many recruits, so imprecise individual messages still steer the colony's foraging effort as a whole toward its richest patches."},
    {"id": "GMAT-RC3", "title": "Default options in retirement saving", "text":
     "When employers enroll workers in a retirement plan automatically, letting them opt out rather than requiring them to sign up, participation rises sharply, in some firms from under half of new hires to over 90 percent. Economists in the behavioral tradition took this as evidence that many workers want to save but fail to act, and that a well-chosen default can help them do what they would choose with more time and attention.\n\n"
     "Later work added a complication. Automatically enrolled workers tended to stay at the default contribution rate, often a modest 3 percent of pay, even though many of them would previously have chosen a higher rate on their own. Some ended up saving less than they would have under the old system. The default, it turned out, set not only whether people saved but how much.\n\n"
     "Plan designers have since experimented with automatic escalation, in which contributions rise by a percentage point each year until they reach a higher ceiling. Early results suggest that most workers accept the increases as readily as they accepted enrollment. If so, the lesson of the first studies needs restating: defaults do not simply rescue people from inaction; they carry the designer's judgment about what people should do, which makes choosing them a responsibility rather than a technicality."},
    {"id": "GMAT-RC4", "title": "Printing and English spelling", "text":
     "It is often said that the printing press fixed English spelling. The claim is half right. Printers did spread uniform spellings faster than scribes ever could, but for more than a century after the first English presses opened in the 1470s, spelling in printed books remained strikingly loose. The same compositor might set a word two ways on one page, and some did so on purpose: before printers took up the practice of widening the spaces between words to fill out a line, adding or dropping a letter was a convenient way to make each line reach the right margin.\n\n"
     "What finally narrowed the variation was less the press itself than the people and institutions around it. Schoolmasters published spelling books that were widely used in teaching; dictionary makers, culminating in Samuel Johnson in 1755, offered word lists that printers could treat as authorities; and large printing houses issued house rules so that work done by many hands would look consistent. The press made a fixed spelling possible, and it made inconsistency more visible, but the decision to fix spelling was a social one."},
    {"id": "GMAT-RC5", "title": "Dynamic pricing and fairness", "text":
     "Economists generally welcome dynamic pricing, in which a seller adjusts prices as demand changes. Higher prices at peak times ration scarce capacity toward the buyers who value it most, and they give sellers a reason to add capacity when it is most needed. Yet customers often react to price increases during a surge with anger that seems out of proportion to the sums involved, and firms that ignore this reaction can pay heavily.\n\n"
     "Research on perceived fairness helps explain the anger. People tend to judge a price against a reference point, usually the price they are used to paying, and they accept increases that seem to reflect a seller's higher costs far more readily than increases that seem to exploit a buyer's urgent need. A snow shovel that costs more after a storm violates this norm, since the shop's costs have not changed; a shovel that costs more because steel prices rose does not.\n\n"
     "Firms have learned to work around the norm rather than against it. Some present the normal price as the peak price and offer discounts in quiet periods, which shifts the reference point. Others tie surges to visible costs, such as paying drivers more to come out in bad weather. Neither tactic changes the economics of dynamic pricing, but each changes how customers read it."},
    {"id": "GMAT-RC6", "title": "Finding planets by transit", "text":
     "Most of the planets known outside our solar system were found by the transit method: a telescope watches thousands of stars at once and records the slight, regular dimming that occurs when a planet passes in front of its star. The method has been remarkably productive, but it sees the galaxy through a narrow window. A transit can be observed only when a planet's orbit is aligned almost edge-on to our line of sight, and the closer a planet orbits its star, the more likely that alignment is. A planet orbiting close in also transits more often, so a short survey can catch several passes and confirm the signal.\n\n"
     "As a result, early catalogs were crowded with large planets on tight orbits, and some astronomers briefly concluded that such planets were common. Statistical corrections have since reversed much of that impression. Once the odds of detection are accounted for, close-orbiting giant planets turn out to be rare, accompanying perhaps one star in a hundred, while smaller planets on wider orbits are far more numerous than the raw counts suggest. The lesson extends beyond astronomy: a sample tells us about the world only after we understand how the sample was drawn."},
    {"id": "GMAT-RC7", "title": "Reviving a language", "text":
     "Efforts to revive endangered languages have often begun with documentation: linguists record the last fluent speakers, write grammars and compile dictionaries. Such work preserves a language for scholars, but it rarely produces new speakers. A language survives only if children acquire it, and children acquire languages they hear used for real purposes, not languages they meet in archives.\n\n"
     "The revival of Hawaiian illustrates the difference. By the 1980s, fewer than fifty children spoke the language. Parents and teachers then founded preschools in which caregivers used only Hawaiian, followed by immersion schools in which the whole curriculum was taught in it. Since then thousands of students have been educated in Hawaiian, and a generation of parents is raising children who hear it at home.\n\n"
     "The immersion model is demanding. It requires teachers fluent enough to teach mathematics or science in the language, along with books and technical terms that may not yet exist. Documentation, far from being made obsolete, becomes indispensable at this stage, since the grammars and recordings that once served only scholars now supply the raw material from which teachers build lessons."},
    {"id": "GMAT-RC8", "title": "The paint tube and Impressionism", "text":
     "Accounts of Impressionism often credit a humble invention, the collapsible metal paint tube, introduced in the 1840s. Before it, painters stored ground pigment mixed with oil in pig bladders that leaked and dried out, which made sustained work outdoors awkward. The tube kept paint fresh and portable, and Renoir is reported to have said that without it there would have been no Impressionism.\n\n"
     "Such remarks deserve some skepticism. Painters had sketched outdoors in oils long before the tube, and many Impressionist canvases that look spontaneous were finished, or painted entirely, in the studio. Yet the tube mattered in a less obvious way. The manufacturers who sold paint in tubes also sold new, intense pigments, such as cobalt violet and synthetic ultramarine, ready-mixed and consistent from one tube to the next. Painters could therefore lay bright colors directly on the canvas without first grinding and mixing them, a practice that helps account for the high-keyed palette the movement is known for."},
    {"id": "GMAT-RC9", "title": "Network effects", "text":
     "A platform such as a ride-hailing app or an online marketplace becomes more valuable to each user as more users join, since buyers attract sellers and sellers attract buyers. This network effect is often said to produce winner-take-all markets: once one platform leads, newcomers cannot match its value, and the leader's position becomes unassailable.\n\n"
     "The history of such markets is less tidy. Several once-dominant platforms lost their lead within a few years, among them early social networks and, in some countries, online auction sites. Two features help explain why network effects protect some leaders and not others. The first is multihoming: if users can easily use several platforms at once, as many drivers and riders do with competing ride-hailing apps, a newcomer need not pull users away from the leader to attract them. The second is locality. A ride-hailing network is valuable only within a city, so a rival can win one city at a time, whereas a messaging network that connects people across the world offers a newcomer no such foothold.\n\n"
     "Network effects, then, raise the cost of entry, but whether they lock up a market depends on how users behave and on how far the network's value extends."},
    {"id": "GMAT-RC10", "title": "Coral heat tolerance", "text":
     "Corals bleach when water that is too warm causes them to expel the algae living in their tissues, which supply most of their food. A coral that stays bleached for long starves. For years it was assumed that each coral species had a fixed heat tolerance, so that a given amount of warming would kill reefs in a predictable way.\n\n"
     "Surveys after recent marine heat waves suggest more flexibility. Corals of a single species on reefs that regularly experience brief spikes in temperature often survive heat that kills the same species on steadier reefs. Experiments point to two sources of this tolerance: some corals host strains of algae that cope better with heat, and corals exposed to moderate stress appear to adjust their own cellular responses.\n\n"
     "The finding is hopeful but limited. Tolerance gained in these ways raises the temperature a coral can survive by perhaps one degree Celsius, while the warming projected for many reefs this century is larger. Flexibility may buy reefs time; it does not make them safe."},
]

